                <pb facs="0190" n="92" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg92"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.801 Search/9: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-03-04T12:00:00+08:00">March 4,
                        1930—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 4—5:30 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>169. Legation’s 166, March 3, 1 p.m. Following from Hankow: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“March 3, 3 p.m. My February 27, noon. General Ho Chien has now replied
                            stating that the search is for the purpose of suppressing movements of
                            Communists. This statement does not harmonize with statements made by
                            the soldiers themselves who assert that it is for the purpose of
                            enforcing embargo against shipment of rice out of Hunan.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
